A SOC analyst should never directly open the URL. Instead, the investigation must be performed using passive and controlled methods.

First, the analyst should defang and parse the URL to extract key indicators such as the domain (meddefense-health.com), subdomain (portal-update), and path (/verify). This helps identify patterns such as impersonation of trusted healthcare or internal services.

Next, the domain should be checked in threat intelligence platforms (e.g., internal TI, VirusTotal, URL reputation feeds, HC3/ISAC intelligence) to determine if it is flagged as malicious, recently registered, or associated with phishing campaigns.

The analyst should then perform WHOIS and domain registration analysis to evaluate domain age, registrar, and ownership patterns. Newly registered domains or privacy-protected registrations often increase phishing suspicion.

DNS analysis should also be performed to review A, MX, and NS records to identify suspicious hosting infrastructure, shared hosting with known malicious domains, or fast-changing IP addresses.

If available, the URL should be submitted to a remote sandbox or URL scanning service that fetches and analyzes the page externally, allowing safe observation of redirects, login forms, and scripts without exposing the analyst’s device.

Finally, the analyst should correlate the URL with email metadata such as sender address, authentication results, and similar alerts in the environment to determine if it is part of a broader phishing campaign.

At no point should the URL be opened directly in a browser or on an endpoint used for production work.
